228: ## SP.3. The outer cap: the actual product arrow
229: 
230: The outer coordinate change is the literal map of F56
231: \[
232:  A_\rho=\begin{pmatrix}1&-\rho\\1&\rho\end{pmatrix},
233:  \qquad
234:  \mathcal B_\rho(P)=
235:        \frac{A_\rho P A_\rho^*}{\operatorname{tr}(A_\rho P A_\rho^*)}.
236:                                                                \tag{SP.12}
237: \]
238: Here the closed three-ball is the positive semidefinite Hermitian
239: matrices of trace one. Its boundary consists of rank-one matrices.
240: This is a periodic diffeomorphism of the ball, including its centre,
241: and its boundary map is exactly \(r\). We use its actual product
242: extension on the surgery collar, rather than replacing the normal
243: frame by its derivative.
244: 
245: The collar in P3K.40 and F56 is the literal first-resolution product
246: \((h,q_H)\), where \(h\) is the base sphere coordinate and
247: \(q_H=(1+jZ)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\) is the normalized actual
248: Cox quaternion. The physical circle acts by
249: \(q_H\mapsto q_H e^{-i\theta}\). Thus on the normal sphere
250: cover the exact outer cap map is
251: \[
252:  (P,q_H,\rho)\longmapsto
253:                   (\mathcal B_\rho(P),q_H,\rho)
254:  \quad\text{on }D^3\times S^3\times S^1.                 \tag{SP.13}
255: \]
256: It is a periodic diffeomorphism, is equivariant for that same circle,
257: and restricts to the actual base-coordinate change on the boundary.
258: Its quotient is
259: \((\mathcal B_\rho,\operatorname{Id}_{S^2})\). It therefore
260: extends both the actual Cox normal frame and the meridian circle
261: line over the product cap. This is precisely the product surgery
262: collar used in F56: its second factor is the actual Hopf normal
263: sphere, and (SP.13) holds that factor, with its orientation, fixed.
264: 
265: In particular (SP.13) keeps the full determinant winding. For
266: clarity the exact determinant discrepancy can be written down.
267: The Hom arrow induced by (SP.8) takes the Cartier frame \(xy\)
268: of \(L\) to \(y^2\) times the standard \(\mathcal O(-2)\)
269: frame. Under the standard canonical identification this is
270: \[
271:                   y^2\,dZ=2\rho\,ds.                    \tag{SP.14}
272: \]
273: Thus the actual Cox arrow has the factor
274: \(\det A_\rho=2\rho\) relative to the cotangent inverse.
275: Its winding is one. We neither remove it nor identify (SP.13) with
276: the tangent derivative of \(\mathcal B_\rho\). It remains in
277: the Cartier-to-Cox coordinate matrix (SP.9) and hence in the clutch
278: computed below. The earlier attempted derivative comparison that
279: omitted this factor is preserved as a superseded unpromoted attempt.
280: 
281: All coordinates in (SP.13) are the actual coordinates already
282: matched in F56 and P3K.40. In particular we make no additional
283: replacement of \(q_H\) by a Tate-scaled quaternion. The winding
284: \(-6\) of \(q_T/q\) is retained by those matched charts.
285: Equations (SP.11a)--(SP.11b) use the physical \(q\) in those
286: charts; the nonzero factor \(q_T/q\) remains in the period
287: identification of the adjacent cell. Replacing that period by one
288: with factor one would change the marking and is not done here.
289: The original source and line unit gauges used in F56 extend over
290: the full source disc. Their displayed logarithms give extending
291: isotopies on the same normal collar. The induced isotopies of its
292: boundary extend over the product cap by extending their invariant
293: vector fields with a collar cutoff; averaging preserves the
294: physical circle action. This carries these actual gauges in
295: (SP.13), rather than changing the cap framing.
296: 
297: ## SP.4. The relative second Chern number and the normal sphere bundle
298: 
299: Cap \(W\) by the inner four-ball in its projection-normal frame
300: and the outer \(D^3\times S^1\) in its actual Cox frame. The
301: resulting base and spinor bundle will be denoted by
302: \(\overline W\) and \(\overline E\). The base orientation is
303: \[
304:  \overline W=(S^2\times D^2)\cup(D^3\times S^1)
305:                       =\partial(D^3\times D^2).
306:                                                                \tag{SP.15}
307: \]
308: The first piece has its complex orientation, and the second has
309: minus its displayed product orientation. Thus the boundary of the
310: outer cap is the negative of
311: \(K=\mathbf P^1\times S^1\), with \(K\) oriented as the
312: boundary of \(N\).
313: 
314: The bundle \(E\) extends across the inner ball and is a trivial
315: \(SU(2)\) bundle over \(N\simeq S^2\). Choose a global frame
316: agreeing there with the Cartier frame. Such agreement can be made
317: on the ball because the difference of two extending frames is
318: itself an extending gauge. Compare this frame with the Cox frame on
319: \(W\). On the inner boundary its coordinate matrix is (SP.9).
320: Since this comparison extends over \(W\), its degree on the outer
321: boundary \(K\) is the same as its degree on the inner sphere in
322: its positive complex-domain orientation.
323: 
324: We calculate that degree with its precise trace convention. The
325: first column of (SP.9) is \((y,x)\); the map
326: \((s,\rho)\mapsto(y,x)\) is complex linear with nonzero
327: determinant \(-2\). Hence its normalized boundary map has degree
328: one for the complex first-column orientation. At the identity the
329: positive tangent matrices in that orientation are
330: \[
331:  I=\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\0&-i\end{pmatrix},\quad
332:  J=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
333:  K_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}.
334:                                                                \tag{SP.16}
335: \]
336: They satisfy \(IJ=-K_0\) and \(\operatorname{tr}(IJK_0)=2\).
337: Consequently the right Maurer--Cartan form has
338: \[
339:            \int_K\operatorname{tr}(dg\,g^{-1})^3=24\pi^2.
340:                                                                \tag{SP.17}
341: \]
342: This explicitly fixes the sign; the quaternion convention with the
343: opposite group orientation cannot be substituted in (SP.17).
344: 
345: Equation (SP.9) is a coordinate arrow from the frame on \(N\) to
346: the cap frame. A connection that is zero in the first frame is
347: therefore \(-dg\,g^{-1}\) in the latter frame near their boundary.
348: Writing \(\omega=dg\,g^{-1}\), we have
349: \(d\omega=\omega^2\) and
350: \[
351:  \operatorname{tr}\bigl((-\omega)d(-\omega)
352:                     +\tfrac23(-\omega)^3\bigr)
353:                     =\tfrac13\operatorname{tr}(\omega^3).
354: \]
355: Extend this connection into the cap with a collar cutoff. Stokes'
